# general things to ignore
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
*.egg
*.py[cod]
__pycache__/
*.so
*~
.vscode/

# due to using tox and pytest
.tox

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Virtual environments -- hundreds of MB, and machine-specific. Everyone
# rebuilds their own from requirements.txt.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
env/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Datasets. NEVER commit these.
#
# casting_data/ is the downloaded archive (~7,300 images), and data/ and
# data_small/ are copies of it built by prepare_data.py. Together they are
# several hundred MB. Anyone can regenerate all of it in two commands:
#
#     python -c "from ManufacturingNet.datasets import CastingData; CastingData()"
#     python prepare_data.py casting_data
#     python make_clean_split.py          # -> data_clean/
#     python check_rotations.py           # -> rotation_duplicates.csv
#     python make_grouped_split.py        # -> data_grouped/, no casting
#                                         #    on both sides of the split
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
casting_data/
data/
data_clean/
data_grouped/
data_small/
raw/
*.zip

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stage-two dataset, built by build_defect_classes.py. It is a COPY of the
# defect images already in data_clean/, sorted into two folders -- roughly
# 4,200 files that regenerate in under a minute:
#
#     python build_defect_classes.py
#
# The cluster_assignments.csv and contact sheets under defect_types/ ARE
# tracked: those are the evidence for how the split was made.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
defect_classes/

# Stage-two folders built by make_defect_type_folders.py -- four copies of the
# 200 labelled castings, one per defect question. Regenerates in seconds from
# defect_types/blind_key_r2.csv plus your my_labels CSV, both of which ARE
# tracked. The labels are the irreplaceable part, not the folders.
defect_type_front/

# The blind labelling sheet embeds 100 full-size castings as base64 and runs
# to several MB. blind_key.csv beside it is the part worth keeping.
defect_types/blind_labels*.html

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trained model weights. model_parameters.pth is ~217 MB -- GitHub rejects any
# file over 100 MB outright, so committing this does not just bloat the repo,
# it blocks the push entirely. Use GitHub Releases or a shared drive if a
# trained model needs sharing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pth
*.pt
*.ckpt

# Extracted feature matrices (discover_defect_types.py). ~4,200 castings x
# 4096 dimensions is roughly 69 MB, and it regenerates in under a minute.
# The contact sheet and cluster_assignments.csv beside it ARE worth keeping.
*.npy

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plots written to the repo root by the training loop on every run. The same
# data lives in results/<run>/summary.csv in a form you can actually use.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/loss.png
/accuracy.png

# NOTE: results/ is deliberately NOT ignored. Each run writes a small
# timestamped folder (JSON + CSV, well under 1 MB) that is the reproducible
# record of what was run and what came out. That is exactly the kind of thing
# a capstone wants in version control.
